(e) clean inking slab if dirty;
(f) distribute ink evenly on inking slab by rolling with
fingerprint roller;
(g) wash and dry fingers thoroughly;
(h) in cases where persons perspire profusely, it is
advisable that the officer taking the prints, dries,
inks and prints each finger one at a time;
(i) the officer taking the prints must be in control at all
times;
(j) roll fingers from the end of the nail on one side to the
end of the nail on the other side. The inking of the
finger must go a little beyond the nail joint;
(k) be sure that the impressions are recorded in the cor-
rect order as per fingerprint slip;
(l) use even pressure when taking the fingerprints;
(m) if amputation or deformity makes it impossible to
obtain perfect impressions, submit the best that can
be obtained with a memo stapled on, explaining the
circumstances and also a note made on the particu-
lar space(s) allotted for the fingers;
(n) both the officer taking the prints and the person
being fingerprinted must sign the completed form;
(o) the fingerprint form is to be completed with all
descriptions and the purpose for fingerprinting;
(p) no erasures, overwriting, obliterations or liquid
paper are to be used on the fingerprint form or cer-
tificate; 
(q) the column marked “Commissioner of Police” on the
certificate shall not be signed by any officer other
than a First Division Officer designated by the
Senior Superintendent Criminal Investigation
Department/Criminal Records Office; and
